---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, GITHUB_URL, LEGAL_UPDATED } from '../config';
---

<Page
  title="Terms of Use | AnyAirDrop.com"
  description="The rules for using AnyAirDrop.com: what the service is, what you are responsible for, and what we cannot promise."
  heading="Terms of use"
  lead="These terms are short on purpose. By using AnyAirDrop.com you agree to them, so please read them once."
  updated={LEGAL_UPDATED}
>
  <h2>What AnyAirDrop.com is</h2>
  <p>
    AnyAirDrop.com is a free tool that helps two devices send files to each other through their web browsers. It
    introduces the devices to each other, and the files then travel between them, directly or through an encrypted
    relay. We do not host, store or review your files. How the service handles information is described in the
    <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Who can use it</h2>
  <p>
    You may use AnyAirDrop.com if you are old enough to agree to these terms where you live, and at least 13 years old.
    If you are using it for a company or another person, you confirm you may do so on their behalf.
  </p>

  <h2>You are responsible for your files</h2>
  <p>You decide what you send and what you accept. When you use AnyAirDrop.com you agree not to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Send anything illegal, or anything you have no right to share, such as content that breaches someone’s copyright or privacy.</li>
    <li>Send malware, or files meant to harm a device or a person.</li>
    <li>Send files to people who have not asked for them, or use the service to harass, scam or threaten anyone.</li>
    <li>Interfere with the service, overload it, probe it for weaknesses without permission, or get around its limits.</li>
    <li>Use the relay for anything other than moving files between the people taking part in a transfer.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>We cannot see what is sent</h2>
  <p>
    Files go from one device to the other, so we cannot check them and we are not responsible for what people send each
    other. Only accept files from people you know and trust, and treat unexpected files with the same care as an unexpected
    email attachment. Accepting a file is your decision.
  </p>

  <h2>Trusted devices</h2>
  <p>
    You can mark devices as trusted so they find each other on any network. You are responsible for the devices that
    hold those keys and for removing a trusted device you no longer control. If you turn on the option to add devices
    automatically after a transfer, or to accept files from a device without asking, you accept the risks that come with it.
  </p>

  <h2>The service can change</h2>
  <p>
    AnyAirDrop.com is free and provided as it is. We may change it, limit it or stop it at any time, and we may block
    anyone who misuses it. Speed depends on your devices, your Wi-Fi, your VPN and your browser, and we cannot promise a
    particular speed or that a transfer will always succeed.
  </p>

  <h2>No warranty, and limits on our liability</h2>
  <p>
    We do not promise that AnyAirDrop.com will be uninterrupted, error free or suitable for a particular purpose. To the
    extent the law allows, we are not liable for lost or damaged files, for anything you receive from another person, or for
    other losses that come from using the service. Nothing in these terms limits any right you have by law that cannot be
    limited, such as liability for something we cause by fraud.
  </p>

  <h2>Independence and trademarks</h2>
  <p>
    AnyAirDrop.com is an independent project. It is not affiliated with, endorsed by or sponsored by Apple Inc. or any other
    device maker. Names and trademarks mentioned belong to their owners.
  </p>

  <h2>Open source</h2>
  <p>
    The code is released under the MIT license and is available on <a href={GITHUB_URL}>GitHub</a>. That license covers the
    code. These terms cover your use of the service on this website.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes to these terms</h2>
  <p>
    We may update these terms. The date at the top shows when they last changed, and using the service after a change means
    you accept the new version. If part of these terms turns out to be unenforceable, the rest still applies.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>Questions about these terms: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, or see the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>
</Page>
